  "id": "Compound::DB01160 - Gene::5737",
  "verbalization": "Dinoprost Tromethamine is implicated in multiple adverse effects, including uterine rupture, venospasm, and chemical phlebitis, all of which are mediated through Carboprost Tromethamine. These effects are linked to the binding of Carboprost Tromethamine to Ptgfr, a receptor involved in the regulation of prostaglandin signaling. The shared pathway involves the activation of a P-Block Molecular Entity, which is a common structural feature among prostaglandin analogs, suggesting a conserved mechanism of action. The evidence from the three paths converges on the notion that Dinoprost Tromethamine exerts its effects via Carboprost Tromethamine, which in turn binds to Ptgfr, indicating a direct, mechanistic link between the drug and the receptor. The integration of these findings highlights a potential role for Ptgfr in the pathophysiology of the adverse effects associated with Dinoprost Tromethamine, supported by a shared molecular pathway and receptor engagement.",
